.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_TOTAL_CAPACITY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity \- get total capacity for a size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the total number of entries in the pool that serves the given size.
It does not count a dynamic allocation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool to query
size  Size in bytes to query (maps to a specific sub-pool)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns total capacity for this size
Returns 0 on error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It returns 0 if size is invalid, which means 0 or above largest_size
It rounds size up to the next power-of-two pool
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false

The count that this reports for a size is the count that the tier was built
for. You can always get that many entries of that size. A tier that is large
enough to keep a cache for each thread allocates a reserve beyond that
count. Concurrent callers can therefore hold a few more than this at one
time, for a short while. See ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) for the full
account.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3)
